libairspy: wait for cancelled transfers before freeing or reusing them

A stop cancelled the transfers, joined the transfer and consumer threads, and
handled events once with a zero timeout. libusb calls a cancelled transfer back
later with LIBUSB_TRANSFER_CANCELLED and owns it and its buffer until then, so
transfers were usually still in flight when airspy_stop_rx() returned.
airspy_close() then freed them, a restart (airspy_start_rx() after a stop, as a
retune does) resubmitted them, and airspy_set_packing() cancelled and freed
them back to back: freeing a transfer libusb still owns is undefined
behaviour, and libusb refuses to submit one as busy. A failed submission at
start left the transfers already submitted the same way.

The device now counts the transfers libusb owns (__atomic, since a callback
runs on whichever thread handles events). A start reserves the whole batch
before the first submission, and a failed submission gives back its own slot
and those of the transfers never submitted. The callback keeps the slot when
it resubmits; any other outcome (stopping, a failed status or resubmission)
gives it back as its last touch of the device. The stop's single poll is
replaced by a wait: cancel the transfers and handle events until the count is
zero, with no timeout, since libusb guarantees each callback. Each pass
cancels again, in case a callback on another thread resubmitted before it saw
the stop; a poll that keeps failing backs off 1 ms instead of spinning.

A stream that ended by itself (a failed transfer or event poll, or the sample
callback asking to stop) left its threads unjoined and its other transfers in
flight. airspy_start_rx() and a packing change now reap it first, as a stop
would, before they reset the converters, resubmit or free anything.

streaming and stop_requested become C11 atomics. The caller's stop, the
transfer thread, the consumer thread and the callbacks all read and write
them, which was a data race on plain volatile fields; ThreadSanitizer reports
it as soon as a test drives the library's own threads.

The vendored copy builds with clang for Android; the __atomic builtins and
_Atomic are GCC/clang C11 and are not meant for upstream's MSVC build.

